making a DAP from the xperia E C1505 (w/ last.fm scrobbling)%
it's like a cool mp3 player&
 /* i start this article at 7:15 in a coffee shop on the quiet exeter high street, from memory. forgive any lapses/mistakes!! /* 
 (**introduction**) 
i was recently given (lent) an old phone, the sony xperia E (C1505). it's fun, but useless in the modern day. it runs android jellybean (4.1) as stock so very few modern apps actually run on it, and most of the legacy editions that do are either deprecated or broken. 
<IMG="assets\techstuff\xperia\z_sony-xperia-e-1.jpg"> 
so, what was there to do with it? \n i've always wanted an mp3 player but the modern ones are stupid expensive and the old ones are useless, lack features and have little storage. 
the benefit, i realised, of transforming a tiny little phone into a DAP is that it has every feature an mp3 player could need, and more (i.e. a browser, gps, camera). (;) so, the scheme of work was this: \n 
first of all, the stock sony ROM is full of junk and really slow, so i had to find and install a new one (after working out how). \n 
then, i had to clear out any junk that might come with the new ROM. \n 
penultimately, i would need to populate it with the applications i need for my purposes. i wanted a browser, a media player and a way to sync my listening history to last.fm (follow me ! a:https://last.fm/eloisverycool ). this was all by far the trickiest step. \n 
the final step was to obviously transfer my music across. (;) 
 (**changing_the_ROM**) 
changing a rom on an android phone is meant to be easy, and, once i had gathered all the files, it was. unfortunately, the roms are so hard to find. any developer interest in this phone died down circa 2015, with only a couple of attempts to revive some forums made in the last few years. this would be fine, except for the fact that most links and shared files had all died, making finding a functional ROM very tricky. yet, this isnt even the first step: 
(**-_unlocking_the_bootloader**) 
unlocking a bootloader is fine. i've done it before (but never installed any roms), and it's a very well guided process. sony themselves provides helpful tools. \n 
you need a special code and ADB (the tool for interacting with your phone from a computer). \n 
getting the code is easy. dial '' *#06# '' to get your IMEI, and follow the steps and enter the IMEI at a:https://developer.sony.com/open-source/aosp-on-xperia-open-devices/get-started/unlock-bootloader . however, and this might just be an issue on my end, but i really struggled to get the drivers working for ADB. my computer couldnt communicate with the phone, and windows would not install the drivers. i downloaded some online, and pointed the device manager right at them, and it still "couldn't find them". \n enter flashtool: flashtool is a very helpful tool (shocking), and i'll explain its use later, but most importantly? it comes with the usb drivers, as a nice helpful installer. \n 
it still wouldnt work! after a bit of searching about, i discovered it was because the drivers were unverified or unsigned in some way. the remedy to this was to boot windows in a special mode where it doesn't care for such security measures. 
hold shift while you restart your computer. as it reboots, you should be met with a new screen. navigate down troubleshoot > advanced options > startup settings and then press f7. herein the installer worked perfectly, and the phone was recognised. 
finally, to unlock the bootloader: \n 
enable usb debugging on your device (developer options) \n 
shut off the c1505 \n 
plug one end of the usb cable into the computer, and as you plug the other into the phone you have to HOLD volume up. this puts it in fastboot mode \n 
navigate to your platform tools (ADB) folder, and open a terminal window at that location. enter the command ''' fastboot oem unlock 0x(the code from the sony website) ''' \n 
perfect!! 
the next step: to add a rom, you need a recovery mode. for a recovery mode (there isnt one included with the c1505 stock kernel), you need to flash a different kernel. 
a word of warning: when i unlocked the bootloader, the phone bootlooped. this resolved itself when i installed the new kernel (next), but the loop is easily stopped by taking out the battery (which is straightforward). 
(**-_flashing_a_new_kernel**) 
this was, again, fairly easy. it just took time to find a working kernel file. \n 
at this point, i found this (short) thread on xda : a:https://xdaforums.com/t/archive-xperia-e-rom-kernel.4637526/ \n a repository of roms and kernels, with fully alive links! a kernel i had seen mentioned quite a lot was the zues [sic] (zeus??) kernel, so i went with that, the version containing TWRP. with the flashtool i mentioned earlier, it was very easy to install. (you actually can supposedly unlock the bootloader very easily with flashtool, but i failed once and didnt attempt again, could be worth trying instead of the arduity above) \n 
download the kernel, connect your phone in fastboot, click the lighting bolt in flashtool and select the kernel file. let it flash, and that's all. \n 
(**-_installing_the_ROM**) 
the first thing was to pick a rom. i tested this with something i knew would work, cyanogenmod 11 (all roms mentioned here are linked in that xda post), which installed flawlessly. 
using ADB, you push the zip file to the phone. for me, this looked like (in the same folder as the zipped rom) '' adb push rom.zip /sdcard/ '' \n 
you then boot to recovery (reboot it and keep pressing volume up when the sony logo appears), and select install. choose the zip you just pushed through adb and follow the instructions. \n 
because it installed well, i thought everything was fine. however, in CM11, the camera doesn't work. it also didnt feel brilliant to use, so i went about trying other roms. \n 
i tried about 5, all either failing to install or just not working, until i came across ambyROM. i honestly don't know (and cant find) any information about it, except that i think it's based on cyanogenmod and the camera works. \n i flashed both the V3 and the V5 update, which all worked. ambyROM was, however, filled with some junk. luckily, (almost) none of the junk is demarcated as a system app, so it can all just be uninstalled. (;) 
(**finding_software**) 
this definitely took the longest. not many things work on android jellybean anymore, and finding archives can be a pain. \n 
i started with an old version of VLC, which did succeed in playing music, but didn't have a folder view. i wasn't too fussed about this, so ignored it. as for syncing to lastfm: panoscrobbler, the arguably best mobile scrobbler does NOT work on android <5. i tried the latest working version of simple lastfm scrobbler (SLS), but that couldn't get the permissions it wanted and later hung on the authentication screen. \n next, i tried scrobbledroid. it didn't really give me any feedback, so i have no clue if i set it up right, but it didn't submit anything to lastfm (from VLC) \n giving up, i went back to SLS, but an older version. this one logged in fine, and didn't request any permissions. it actually submitted a "now playing" HTTP POST but never scrobbled anything. it also stopped working after one song. \n 
it was so frustrating. lastfm has been around for like 20 years, and there wasn't anything that worked well. \n 
i tried a new avenue: music apps with built in support. of which, for jellybean, i could find ONE. it goes by the name of jukebox player, and is quite feature rich and well built. most importantly, it has a scrobbler built in. it worked almost perfectly. while testing, i found out it doesn't cache scrobbles, i.e. if a song is played without internet connection, it just gets forgotten about. useless!
moving on again... i finally found out about a new player: poweramp. 
poweramp can interface directly to SLS, which works just as expected. additionally, it has a cool folder view and lock screen display, as well as built in fx. one caveat of poweramp is that it is paid, with a 2 week grace period to try it out. i am currently in that grace period, and i have read that on older releases / roms (such as those without google play installed) the grace period just times out and makes no attempt to make you buy the product. i'll be back to update this after the time has passed. \n 
the final step was to give the illusion that the phone wasn't a phone at all. poweramp should be visible when you unlock the phone, etc. etc. \n 
this is easily achieved using MacroDroid. set the triggers to be unlocking the phone, connecting headphones, booting the phone (and so on) and set the action to be booting SLS and poweramp. this works very well. this also ensures that SLS is always on, scrobbling, as SLS can't be made to natively start on start-up. \n 
that's basically it. finally was moving music to the phone, which could be just pushed to the SD card through ADB (after converting to mp3). 
 
(**closing_remarks**) 
i've been using this device for music for the last few days (maybe a week), and it works perfectly. i have had no issues. one quirk with the phone is that the volume slider has 50 increments, as opposed to the normal 10 or so, meaning to significantly change volume you have to click the button a large number of times. i dont know if this comes from the ROM, from android 4.1.1 or some other source. 



 (**specs_and_TLDR**) 
see below the information regarding exactly what is installed on the phone to get it working \n 
specs:
 ''' 
model: xperia e c1505 single
ROM: AmbyROM v5 (android 4.1.1)
kernel: 3.4.0ZuesJB
software:
    poweramp v2.0.10-build-588-play
    simple last.fm scrobbler v1.4.9
    MacroDroid v3.17.13
 ''' 

steps to reproduce: 
''' 
1. unlock bootloader
2. flash 3.4.0ZuesJB kernel with flashtool
3. transfer AmbyROM v3 and v5 update files
4. boot to recovery and install both v3 and v5, in that order
5. delete the unneeded apps in ambyrom
6. install the software listed above (download apks from uptodown, apkmirror, etc)
7. set up conditions in MacroDroid so that the right apps launch when you need
8. push your music to the SD card 
''' 
